> 
> ### 1. **Public Health Campaigns**
>    - **Example**: Investing in a nationwide smoking cessation program.
>    - **Scenario**: For every dollar spent on the program, society saves $14 in reduced healthcare costs, increased productivity, and improved quality of life due to fewer smoking-related diseases.
> 
> ### 2. **Environmental Restoration**
>    - **Example**: Wetland restoration projects.
>    - **Scenario**: A project that costs $1 million to restore wetlands could yield $14 million in benefits through flood mitigation, improved water quality, and enhanced biodiversity.
> 
> ### 3. **Infrastructure Improvements**
>    - **Example**: Public transportation expansion.
>    - **Scenario**: For every dollar invested in a new light rail system, cities may save $14 over time through reduced traffic congestion, lower greenhouse gas emissions, and improved public health.
> 
> These high BCR projects often yield societal benefits that far surpass their costs, making them prime candidates for funding and support in sustainability efforts.
